# Shell scripts are LF in the repository, always.
#
# Not cosmetics: a shebang that ends in CRLF makes the kernel look for an
# interpreter literally named "bash\r", so the script dies with a
# "bad interpreter" error on Linux and macOS. Windows checkouts run these
# under Git Bash, which tolerates either, so a CRLF script committed from
# Windows breaks only for OTHER people — the worst kind of regression to
# catch, because it looks fine on the machine that introduced it.
#
# This happened: tests/run-all.sh was committed with CRLF in v0.5.5 after
# a script rewrote it preserving the working tree endings. Every other
# tracked file was, and is, LF in the index.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the TypeScript the hooks execute, and for the JSON
# contracts read by both. Keeping them LF also keeps diffs honest: a
# whole-file rewrite hides the real change inside 2000 lines of noise.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
